// @check
// @error A boolean-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// @error A boolean-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// @error A boolean-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// DIAGNOSTIC (D-MAPSPELL): the actionable unsupported-key message fires for a map spelling
// that is NOT the whole annotation — a LIST of boolean-keyed maps, a NULLABLE one, and an
// INTERSECTION whose left operand is one. All three open `{[` and none ends `}`, which is
// the exact width the MAP-NAME grammar's KEY CUT (`mapSpellKeyName`) was deliberately NOT
// gated on: the key ends at its own `]`, so the cut answered `"boolean"` for
// `{[boolean]:i32}[]`, `{[boolean]:i32}|null` and `{[boolean]:i32}&Foo` just as it does for
// the bare `{[boolean]:i32}` that `error-unsupported-key.vl` already pins.
//
// D-MAPKEYDIAG replaced that cut with the parser's SPELLING TREE, and these three lines are
// what pin the replacement's width: they are the ARRAY, UNION and INTERSECTION arms of
// `typecheck.tsMapKeyNodeOf`'s left-spine descent, one line each. Deleting any one arm
// silently downgrades its line to a bare `unknown type`, and
// `error-unsupported-key-not-at-head.vl` pins the other side — the four carriers that must
// NOT descend.
//
// This is the fixture that keeps the width honest. The VALUE cut (`mapSpellValName`) DOES
// take the closing `}`, because a value has no right edge without it; the key does not, and
// a slice that homed both behind one span gate would silently downgrade these two lines to
// a bare `unknown type '{[boolean]:i32}[]'`. Measured before it was written: a divergence
// probe over the whole corpus found ZERO files in which the key cut answers for a name that
// is not a whole map span, so the population this width protects had no witness at all.
//
// The key spelled here is `boolean` because `i32` is now a SUPPORTED key (B6a) — the same
// width, a key the rep still has no hash for.
type Foo = { a: i32 }
let xs: {[boolean]: i32}[] = []
let m: {[boolean]: i32} | null = null
let n: {[boolean]: i32} & Foo = null
